WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
AWS Lambda is serverless compute, but serverless does not remove application security responsibility.

2
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:19.400
A .NET function still validates input, enforces authorization decisions, protects secrets, handles errors safely, and produces logs that are useful without exposing sensitive data.

3
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.120
A Lambda function is shaped by more than handler code.

4
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:37.380
The security result depends on the function code, handler design, event source, IAM role, resource-based policy, deployment package, dependencies, configuration, secrets, networking, logs, and operational process.

5
00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.040
Each of those pieces can change what the function can do and what data it can reach.

6
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:51.200
Lambda functions can process HTTP requests, queue messages, storage events, stream records, scheduled events, and service events.

7
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.620
Each trigger creates a trust boundary.

8
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.600
The function should not assume that data is safe only because it arrived through an AWS integration.

9
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:03.020
It should validate the event and make decisions from trusted context.

10
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:13.060
Security responsibility is shared across developer code, AWS platform configuration, IAM, deployment automation, monitoring, and incident response.

11
00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:24.600
A secure baseline starts by asking what invokes the function, what identity the function runs as, what data it reads or writes, what secrets it receives, and how retries and failures behave.
